      "id": 1,
      "text": "Heritage Park opened in 2004, featuring a Tudor‑style Wickers Building from 1919, a 1917 superintendent cottage, and a restored Interurban trolley car.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynnwood%2C_Washington",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Heritage Park also hosts free tours of the historic trolley and offers a comic book that tells Lynnwood’s early history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lynnwoodwa.gov/Community/Play-Lynnwood/Arts-Culture-and-Heritage/Heritage-Things-to-Do",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lynnwood High School originally sat next to Alderwood Mall before moving a mile east to North Road in northern Bothell in 2008.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynnwood%2C_Washington",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city’s borders run south to Mountlake Terrace, west to Edmonds, north to Picnic Point‑North Lynnwood, and east to Alderwood Manor along State Route 525.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynnwood%2C_Washington",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lynnwood falls within the Seattle–Tacoma media market, receiving news from The Seattle Times and local TV stations like KOMO‑TV and KING‑TV.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynnwood%2C_Washington",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The first civic center buildings, including city hall and the public library, opened in 1971 and later expanded to house a police station and courthouse.",
